# rules: csharp-credential-is-a-literal
# languages: c_sharp
# fixture: csharp/CWE-798.cs
# from: CWE-798 (Use of Hard-coded Credentials), CWE-259
#
# A credential written into the source is a credential in every checkout, every
# build artifact, every branch anyone ever pushed, and the git history forever
# after. Rotating it means finding all of those. The one in the fixture is a
# connection string, which is where most real ones live in .NET.
#
# The name decides it, and getting the name test right is most of the rule.
# `password`, `secret`, `token`, `apikey`, `passwd`, `pwd` and the connection
# string's own `Password=` are what the corpus writes.
#
# `connectionString` is deliberately *not* one of the names, though it was in
# the first draft. A connection string is only a credential when it contains
# one, and most do not: `"Data Source=test.db"` and
# `"Data Source=people;Mode=Memory;Cache=Shared"` are the whole of what a
# SQLite test file assigns to a field called `ConnectionString`, and there were
# nine of them in one directory of efcore. So the connection string is caught
# by reading its *value* for a `Password=` or a `User Id=`, which is what makes
# `$"Data Source={dbPath};Password=test;..."` a finding and the other nine
# not.
#
# Six things that look like credentials and are not had to be excluded, and
# each was found by running this over code nobody wrote it for:
#
#   - a name for the field rather than the value. `PasswordFieldName`,
#     `TokenParameter`, `SecretKeyName` - the string is `"password"`, or
#     `"oauth_token"`, or `"oauth_token_secret"`, the name of a form field or a
#     protocol parameter. All of them are one lower-case word, or several
#     joined by underscores or hyphens, and every one of them *contains the
#     credential word itself*. That pairing is the test, and both halves are
#     needed: "letters only" alone excludes `hunter2`, which is a password, and
#     "contains the word" alone excludes `MyPassword123`, which is also a
#     password - the digits are what tell the two apart, so the exclusion
#     allows letters, spaces and separators and nothing else. efcore's
#     `SqliteConnectionStringBuilder` has a column of these -
#     `PasswordKeyword = "Password"` beside `ModeKeyword = "Mode"` - and the
#     capital letter is why the test is spelled case-insensitively. A secret whose text is nothing but lower-case letters
#     and the word `token` is missed, and has bigger problems than this rule.
#   - a URL. `"https://oauth2.googleapis.com/token"` assigned to
#     `TokenEndpoint` is an endpoint, and the word in the field name is about
#     what you ask for there. ShareX has six of these across its OAuth
#     providers and every one was reported.
#   - an HTTP header name. `Proxy-Authorization`, `X-Api-Key`: capitalised
#     words joined by hyphens, which is a shape no password has.
#   - an empty string or an obvious placeholder. `""` is not a secret, and
#     neither is `"changeme"` or `"your-api-key-here"`.
#   - anything that is not a literal at all. `Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable`
#     in the initialiser is the fix, not the bug, and a field called `Password`
#     is exactly where you would expect to find it.
#
# A field, a const and a local are all `variable_declaration` to this grammar,
# so the single pattern `$T $V = $E;` reaches all three where Java needed three
# separate node types for the same question.
#
# A property with an initialiser is not reached, and it should be - it is a
# common place for exactly this bug:
#
#     public string ApiSecret { get; set; } = "sk_live_9c3f11a2b7";
#
# `$T $V { get; set; } = $E;` compiles and reports itself valid, and the query
# it makes is anchored to a `class_declaration` whose body is that property and
# nothing else. It matches a two line file and no real one. This is the class
# scaffold leaking into the reading rather than being stripped from it - the
# same failure the C# statement readings had, in the one place the fix for
# those does not reach, because the pattern is a member already and so is never
# read as a wrapped statement. It is written down here, and in the fixture,
# rather than worked around.

["$T $V = $E;"] as $declared
| "(?i)pass(word|wd)?|secret|^pwd$|api_?key|token|credential" as $credentialName
| "(?i)^\"[^\"]*\\b(password|pwd|user\\s*id|uid)\\s*=\\s*[^;\"\\s][^;\"]*\"$" as $connectionString
| scan_ast("*.cs"; $declared) as $all
| ( ($all | of($declared) | where_capture("V"; $credentialName))
    + ($all | of($declared) | where_capture("E"; $connectionString)) )
| focus("E")
| where_capture("E"; "^[\"$]")
| where_capture_not("E"; "(?i)^\\$?\"\\s*\"$")
| where_capture_not("E"; "^\"[A-Za-z _-]*(?i:password|passwd|pwd|secret|token|apikey|api_key|credential)[A-Za-z _-]*\"$")
| where_capture_not("E"; "(?i)^\"[a-z][a-z0-9+.-]*://")
| where_capture_not("E"; "^\"[A-Z][A-Za-z0-9]*(-[A-Z][A-Za-z0-9]*)+\"$")
| where_capture_not("E"; "(?i)^\"(changeme|change_me|your[-_ ].*|xxx+|todo|none|null|example|placeholder|<.*>|\\{.*\\})\"$")
| finding("csharp-credential-is-a-literal";
    "this credential is in the source, so it is in every checkout and every "
    + "commit that ever carried it, and rotating it means finding all of them")
| report
